{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>Why have you struck your donkey?</b> For when you saw these signs, you should have inferred that your journey was not approved and would not succeed." "<b>These three times.</b> And how did you harden your neck three times to force the moment?" "<b>Behold, I have come out as an adversary.</b> Behold, I have done everything possible to oppose your journey in this manner, for your own good." "<b>For the way is a terror against me.</b> יָרַט is from ״וְרֶטֶט הֶחֱזִיקָה״ (Jeremiah 49:24), like ״וַתָּרִץ אֶת גֻּלְגָּלְתּוֹ״ (Judges 9:53) from רָצַץ. He said: Behold, I came out as an adversary, and the meaning of the opposition was that the way was made terrifying in all that confronted me, as in ״they did not see the vision, but a great terror fell upon them״ (Daniel 10:7) — and all this did not suffice to terrify you, because you hardened your heart."], :book :numbers, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {3 "Sforno reads רָצַץ (to shatter/break) as the root for both וַתָּרִץ and יָרַט, treating רַט as an Aramaic/variant form of רָצַץ meaning to terrify through crushing force."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:40:47.685399807Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}